  <section class="wrap cv-section" id="experience" aria-labelledby="experience-h">
    <h2 id="experience-h">Experience</h2>
    <div class="cv-item">
      <div class="cv-when">Aug 2020 to present</div>
      <div class="cv-what">
        <strong>Graduate Research Assistant</strong>
        <span class="where">Yale Institute for Network Science and NSF Center for Quantum Networks (2020 to 2025), Yale University</span>
        <ul>
          <li>Characterized the capacity region of quantum entanglement switches with limited memory and decoherence, and designed throughput-optimal scheduling and memory-allocation policies (IEEE JSAC 2024, IEEE Commun. Lett. 2023, IFIP Networking 2023, SIGMETRICS 2023 workshop).</li>
          <li>Compared all-photonic and memory-equipped switch architectures through their rate–fidelity tradeoffs (QCNC 2026), and developed a capacity-driven framework for the design and operation of quantum networks built from such devices.</li>
          <li>Modeled and analyzed large-scale classical networks, including sectorized wireless and sliced networks, designing frameworks that enlarge the throughput region, improve routing efficiency, and support incentive-compatible resource allocation (IEEE ToN 2025, MobiHoc 2023, INFOCOM 2023, CDC 2024).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-item">
      <div class="cv-when">Aug 2024 to Feb 2025</div>
      <div class="cv-what">
        <strong>Applied Scientist Intern</strong>
        <span class="where">Amazon, Transportation Services</span>
        <ul><li>Designed a queueing-theoretic framework and digital-twin simulator for trailer operations, modeling facilities as a network of queues to optimize control policies and reduce trailer leasing and operating costs.</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-item">
      <div class="cv-when">Jul to Aug 2024</div>
      <div class="cv-what">
        <strong>Ph.D. Research Intern</strong>
        <span class="where">Cisco Systems</span>
        <ul><li>Designed a measurement-based distributed quantum computing framework that compiles quantum algorithms into two-qubit gates and measurements while minimizing network communication cost and required quantum memories.</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-item">
      <div class="cv-when">May to Aug 2023</div>
      <div class="cv-what">
        <strong>Ph.D. Research Intern</strong>
        <span class="where">Cisco Systems</span>
        <ul><li>Developed a gate-based distributed quantum computing compiler that translates a quantum circuit into physical operations native to multiple QPUs, jointly optimizing entanglement generation, routing, and teleportation to minimize expected execution time (IEEE ICC 2025, J. Quantum Computing 2025).</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-item">
      <div class="cv-when">Apr 2019 to Aug 2020</div>
      <div class="cv-what">
        <strong>Research Assistant</strong>
        <span class="where">Network Management and Optimal Design Laboratory, National Technical University of Athens</span>
        <ul><li>Modeled multi-user systems that share a common pool of resources using satisfaction games, introduced the <em>minimum efficient satisfaction equilibrium</em>, and designed distributed dynamics that provably converge to it and outperform Nash equilibria in common-pool and wireless resource-allocation use cases (IEEE TAC 2025, IEEE/ACM ToN 2021, IEEE TCNS 2021, WMNC 2019 Best Paper).</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
